    period: "Arthurian Era",
    aliases: "Lady of the Lake; Nimue; Viviane; Niniane",
    history: `The Lady of the Lake is one of the most mysterious and powerful figures in Arthurian legend. Rather than a single character, the title "Lady of the Lake" has been given to several magical women throughout medieval romances, though the most famous are Nimue and Viviane. She is portrayed as a powerful enchantress who dwells within a magical lake or hidden kingdom, possessing wisdom and supernatural abilities beyond those of ordinary mortals.

The Lady of the Lake is best known for giving King Arthur the legendary sword Excalibur after the Sword in the Stone is broken. Rising from the waters, she presents Arthur with the enchanted blade, which becomes the symbol of his rightful kingship. Later, when Arthur is mortally wounded at the Battle of Camlann, she receives Excalibur back as Sir Bedivere throws it into the lake, with a mysterious hand rising from the water to catch the sacred weapon before disappearing beneath the surface.

In many later romances, the Lady of the Lake also raises the young Sir Lancelot after the death of his father, earning him the title "Lancelot du Lac," meaning "Lancelot of the Lake." She teaches him courtesy, chivalry, and the skills that make him the greatest knight of Camelot. Some versions also identify her as the woman who learns Merlin's magic before trapping the great wizard forever inside an enchanted cave or tree. Throughout Arthurian legend, the Lady of the Lake represents mystery, magic, wisdom, and the supernatural forces that guide the destiny of King Arthur and Camelot.`
